# glib-introspection -- GLib-2.0, GObject-2.0, GModule-2.0 and Gio-2.0, as .gir
# and .typelib. Nothing else; the libraries themselves are glib's.
#
# THE CYCLE. g-ir-scanner is a program that links glib in order to introspect a
# library, and glib's own typelibs are produced by g-ir-scanner. Neither can be
# built first. The way out is to compile glib twice: once with introspection
# off, which is the `glib` package, and once with it on after the scanner
# exists, which is this.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE PACKAGE rather than a second pass over the glib recipe. A
# second pass is invisible to anything reading the dependency graph, and CI
# schedules builds in waves derived from that graph -- "build this one twice,
# in the middle" is not something a graph can express. Left as a second pass,
# CI would have built the entire GTK stack against a glib with no typelibs, and
# every introspection-enabled package would have failed for a reason that looks
# nothing like the cause. As a package it is an ordinary node that both the
# Makefile's order and CI's levelling already understand.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT OPTIONAL. gobject-introspection ships only the miscellaneous
# typelibs -- DBus, GL, cairo, xlib, freetype2 and eleven others -- and not
# these four. gjs cannot import Gio without Gio-2.0.typelib, and gnome-shell is
# written in JavaScript.
#
# Same tarball as glib, deliberately: a version skew between the library and
# its own introspection data would be a very confusing bug.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/glib/2.84/glib-2.84.1.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=2b4bc2ec49611a5fc35f86aca855f2ed0196e69e53092bab6bb73396bf30789a
SRC_DIR=glib-2.84.1
